 verify, a pack is not in it or not installed, a pack another needs, or the store's files cannot be written.","table":"table-7"},"1841":{"id":"/docs/servers/http","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"page","content":"HTTP server","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Command line and servers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http"},"1842":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-0","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http","content":"tgd serve answers every query over HTTP as JSON or GeoJSON, with Nominatim-compatible routes, from packs on local disk."},"1843":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-1","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"Start it"},"1844":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-2","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"Native routes"},"1845":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-3","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http#geojson","content":"GeoJSON"},"1846":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-4","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"Nominatim-compatible"},"1847":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-5","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http#errors","content":"Errors"},"1848":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-6","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"Deploying it"},"1849":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-7","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http","content":"`tgd serve` puts the packs behind an HTTP API: every query as a `GET` route, native JSON or\nGeoJSON, and Nominatim-compatible `/search`, `/reverse` and `/lookup` so existing clients work\nunchanged. It is one process with no database: the packs are memory-mapped, and on Nepal it\nanswers about 9,000 requests per second for the heaviest route (a large boundary) and 40,000 to\n90,000 for the others, at 16 connections on one machine (measured\nby `cargo xtask load`, with a budget the load test enforces)."},"1850":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-8","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http","content":"The requests below are part of the command-line tour,\nwhich starts the server on the released pack, sends them with curl, and checks the answers."},"1851":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-9","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--listen <addr:port> · 127.0.0.1:8080 · Where to listen; port 0 picks a free one. Loopback only unless you name another address.","table":"table-0"},"1852":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-10","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--pack <file> · the store's packs · A pack to serve; repeat for several. Given, it replaces the store: only these packs are served, and nothing is followed.","table":"table-0"},"1853":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-11","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--store <dir> · the platform's data directory · Serve the store's installed packs, and follow it as packs are installed or updated.","table":"table-0"},"1854":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-12","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--follow <seconds> · 5 · How often to read the store's journal; 0 never.","table":"table-0"},"1855":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-13","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--timeout <seconds> · 10 · A request taking longer is answered unavailable.","table":"table-0"},"1856":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-14","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--max-connections <n> · 1024 · The most connections kept open; more wait until one closes.","table":"table-0"},"1857":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-15","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--max-in-flight <n> · 256 · The most requests answered at once; one more is answered unavailable at once, with Retry-After: 1.","table":"table-0"},"1858":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-16","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--cors-origin <origin> · none · An origin browsers may call from; repeat for several. CORS is off by default.","table":"table-0"},"1859":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-17","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#start-it","content":"--allow-install · off · Let MCP clients install catalog packs into the store.","table":"table-0"},"1860":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-18","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"Every route is `GET /v1/<query>` with query parameters, and answers\n`{\"results\": [...], \"attribution\": \"...\"}` (or `\"result\"` for one value). `limit` defaults to 10."},"1861":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-19","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/search · Places matching a name, best first, with score.","table":"table-1"},"1862":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-20","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/get · One place; names=1 adds every name with its language.","table":"table-1"},"1863":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-21","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/lookup · Places by ID, in the order asked.","table":"table-1"},"1864":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-22","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/reverse · The nearest places with meters, bearing and inside.","table":"table-1"},"1865":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-23","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/near · The places nearest a place.","table":"table-1"},"1866":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-24","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/admin · The admin units holding the point, country first.","table":"table-1"},"1867":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-25","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/hierarchy · A place's ancestors, parent first.","table":"table-1"},"1868":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-26","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/children · A place's children.","table":"table-1"},"1869":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-27","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/tz · The timezone with its offset, abbreviation and DST flag at t.","table":"table-1"},"1870":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-28","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/elevation · The terrain height, its datum, source and grid spacing.","table":"table-1"},"1871":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-29","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/distance · The great-circle distance and initial bearing.","table":"table-1"},"1872":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-30","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/postal · Postal codes and their places.","table":"table-1"},"1873":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-31","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/boundary · A unit's outline as GeoJSON.","table":"table-1"},"1874":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-32","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/v1/coverage · The catalog's packs that cover a point (a server over a store).","table":"table-1"},"1875":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-33","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/packs · The packs served, with their components, places and attribution.","table":"table-1"},"1876":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-34","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"/health · {\"status\": \"ok\", \"layers\": n}.","table":"table-1"},"1877":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-35","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"Every parameter of every route, with its bounds and default, is in the HTTP API reference."},"1878":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-36","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"Search:"},"1879":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-37","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"Reverse geocoding; `inside` is true when the place's area holds the point:"},"1880":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-38","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"The timezone at a point, at an instant:"},"1881":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-39","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#native-routes","content":"Elevation and distance answer one value, under `result`:"},"1882":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-40","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#geojson","content":"Add `format=geojson` to any route that answers places, and the answer is a FeatureCollection\n(RFC 7946) that MapLibre, Leaflet, OpenLayers and QGIS read directly:"},"1883":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-41","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"`/search`, `/reverse` and `/lookup` speak Nominatim's API for the parameters its clients use,\nin the `json`, `jsonv2` and `geojson` formats (JSONP is refused: use CORS):"},"1884":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-42","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"/search · q, or the structured amenity, street, city, county, state, country, postalcode; limit (at most 40, as Nominatim); countrycodes; viewbox and bounded; addressdetails","table":"table-2"},"1885":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-43","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"/reverse · lat, lon, zoom (how coarse a place to answer); addressdetails (on by default)","table":"table-2"},"1886":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-44","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"/lookup · osm_ids: up to 50 teigeodata IDs, comma-separated","table":"table-2"},"1887":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-45","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"Point a Nominatim client, a geocoder plugin or a script at teigeodata and it works offline:"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-46","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"A conformance corpus of real client requests is checked in CI. Differences from Nominatim, where\nwhat cannot be filled is left out rather than faked:"},"1889":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-47","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"`place_id` is a teigeodata ID (a string); there is no `osm_type`, `osm_id`, `class`, `type` or\n`place_rank`, so `json` and `jsonv2` answer the same objects."},"1890":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-48","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"`boundingbox` is the place's point (south = north, west = east), not its extent; draw an\noutline with the native `/boundary`."},"1891":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-49","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#nominatim-compatible","content":"The address names the admin units by level as the country defines them."},"1892":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-50","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#errors","content":"A failure answers a stable code and an HTTP status:"},"1893":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-51","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#errors","content":"The codes and their statuses are listed in the reference: `invalid_argument` and `out_of_bounds` answer 400 (a query string past the server's bound 414), `not_found` 404, `missing_component` 501, `unavailable` 503 and `internal` 500; `unauthenticated` 401, `forbidden` 403 and `rate_limited` 429 only from a deployment that adds keys, rules or limits."},"1894":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-52","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"**Bind it where you mean to.** It listens on loopback by default; behind a reverse proxy\n(Caddy, nginx), keep it there. With `--listen 0.0.0.0:8080` it answers anyone who can reach\nthe port."},"1895":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-53","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"**Limits are built in.** Every request is bounded (text length, `limit` at most 50, radius,\ntime), so no request can make the server do unbounded work."},"1896":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-54","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"**Floods and slow clients are bounded too.** Connections and requests in flight are capped.\nA request head must arrive within 10 seconds, and an idle HTTP/1 connection is closed after the\nsame time. HTTP/2 peers are pinged every 20 seconds (and dropped when they stop answering) and\nget at most 32 streams each. A request past the in-flight\ncap is refused at once, never queued, so a client should retry after `Retry-After`. A reverse\nproxy in front still does TLS and per-client rate limits."},"1897":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-55","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"**Updates without restarts.** Over a store, the server reads its journal every `--follow`\nseconds and serves newly installed or updated packs; queries in flight finish on the packs\nthey started with."},"1898":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-56","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"**Attribution** comes with every answer, from the packs that answered it; keep it visible\nwhere the data is shown."},"1899":{"id":"/docs/servers/http-57","page_id":"/docs/servers/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/http#deploying-it","content":"**MCP** is served at `/mcp` on the same port (Streamable HTTP): see MCP server."},"1900":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","type":"page","content":"MCP server","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Command line and servers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/mcp"},"1901":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-0","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp","content":"tgd mcp gives AI agents teigeodata's queries as MCP tools, offline, with attribution on every answer."},"1902":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-1","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/mcp#connect-a-client","content":"Connect a client"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-2","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"Tools"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-3","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/mcp#installs","content":"Installs"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-4","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"Try it"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-5","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp","content":"The Model Context Protocol lets an AI agent call tools. `tgd mcp`\ngives it every teigeodata query as a tool: an agent can geocode a place, find what is at a\npoint, read a timezone or a height, and get the right answer from local packs instead of\nguessing, with no API key and no network."},"1907":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-6","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#connect-a-client","content":"The client starts the server itself over standard input and output:"},"1908":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-7","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#connect-a-client","content":"Command `tgd`, arguments `mcp --pack <file>` (repeat `--pack` for several packs, or leave it out\nto serve the store's installed packs). Over HTTP, `tgd serve` also answers MCP at `/mcp`\n(Streamable HTTP) on its port."},"1909":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-8","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#connect-a-client","content":"The server speaks MCP 2026-07-28 and 2025-11-25, and older clients too."},"1910":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-9","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"Every tool but `install` is read-only (`readOnlyHint`), declares its output schema, and returns the same\nJSON as structured content and as text, with the attribution of the packs that answered."},"1911":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-10","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"search · Places matching a name, best first; \"Name, Parent\" and a focus point as in the API.","table":"table-0"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-11","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"reverse · The places nearest a point, with distance and bearing.","table":"table-0"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-12","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"nearby · The places nearest a place.","table":"table-0"},"1914":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-13","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"get_place · One place by its ID, optionally with every name it has.","table":"table-0"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-14","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"lookup · Places by ID, in the order asked.","table":"table-0"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-15","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"admin_at · The admin units holding a point, country first.","table":"table-0"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-16","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"hierarchy · A place's ancestors up to its country.","table":"table-0"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-17","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"children · A place's children, such as a country's provinces.","table":"table-0"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-18","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"timezone · The IANA zone of a place or at a point, with its offset and abbreviation.","table":"table-0"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-19","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"elevation · The terrain height at a point.","table":"table-0"},"1921":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-20","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"postal · Postal codes to places, by prefix, or at a point.","table":"table-0"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-21","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"boundary · A unit's outline: a summary, and the GeoJSON as a resource.","table":"table-0"},"1923":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-22","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"distance · The distance and bearing between two points or places.","table":"table-0"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-23","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"packs · The packs served, with their components and attribution.","table":"table-0"},"1925":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-24","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"coverage · The catalog's packs that cover a point (a server over a store).","table":"table-0"},"1926":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-25","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"install, install_status · Install catalog packs into the store, after the person confirms the size (a server over a store that allows it).","table":"table-0"},"1927":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-26","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"Every argument, bound and answer field is in the MCP tools reference."},"1928":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-27","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#tools","content":"Long answers are paged with signed cursors, so an agent can never be handed more than its context\ncan hold. Packs and places are also **resources**: `teigeodata://dataset/…`,\n`teigeodata://place/…` and `teigeodata://boundary/…`."},"1929":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-28","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#installs","content":"Over a store, `tgd mcp` lists `install` and `install_status`, so an agent that finds a place\noutside the installed packs can offer to download its country. The person confirms the size\nthrough the client (elicitation) before a byte is fetched; with a client that cannot ask, the\ncall installs only up to its own `max_bytes` (100 MB when left out), and a larger download answers\nwith the size and asks the agent to call again with `max_bytes` raised to it. `tgd mcp --no-install` leaves the tools out; `tgd serve` lists\nthem only with `--allow-install`."},"1930":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-29","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"Ask your agent things like:"},"1931":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-30","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"\"What's the timezone and the elevation of Namche Bazaar?\""},"1932":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-31","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"\"Which district and province is 28.2096, 83.9856 in?\""},"1933":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-32","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"\"List the provinces of Nepal with their ISO codes.\""},"1934":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-33","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"\"How far is Pokhara from Kathmandu as the crow flies?\""},"1935":{"id":"/docs/servers/mcp-34","page_id":"/docs/servers/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/servers/mcp#try-it","content":"The agent calls `search`, `timezone`, `elevation`, `admin_at`, `children` and `distance`, and\nanswers from the packs, citing their sources."},"1936":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","type":"page","content":"A GPS fix to a place","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place"},"1937":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","content":"Turn a latitude and longitude into \"331 m SE of Narayanhiti Royal Palace, Kathmandu, Bagmati\": the nearest places with distance and bearing, and the admin units that hold the point."},"1938":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-nearest-places","content":"Find the nearest places"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-units-that-hold-the-point","content":"Find the units that hold the point"},"1940":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#put-the-label-together","content":"Put the label together"},"1941":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#everything-within-a-radius","content":"Everything within a radius"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#next","content":"Next"},"1943":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","content":"A phone reports a latitude and longitude; the user wants to read where they are. Two queries\nanswer it: **reverse** gives what is *near* the point, and **admin units at a point** gives what\n*holds* it. Together they make a label such as \"331 m SE of Narayanhiti Royal Palace · Kathmandu, Bagmati\"."},"1944":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-nearest-places","content":"`reverse` gives the places nearest the point, nearest first, each with `meters` and `bearing`\n(degrees clockwise from north, from the place to the point)."},"1945":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-nearest-places","content":"<TourCode region=\"reverse\" />"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-nearest-places","content":"The first row is the \"near\" part of the label. To say \"north-east of\", turn the bearing into\none of eight directions: `[\"N\", \"NE\", \"E\", \"SE\", \"S\", \"SW\", \"W\", \"NW\"][round(bearing / 45) % 8]`."},"1947":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-units-that-hold-the-point","content":"`admin_at` tests the point against the admin boundaries and answers every unit it is inside,\ncountry first. Near a border, the answer is the side the point is on, not the nearest centre."},"1948":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#find-the-units-that-hold-the-point","content":"<TourCode region=\"admin\" />"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#put-the-label-together","content":"Take the nearest place with its distance and direction, then the units from the lowest level\nup, leaving out the ones a reader does not need (most apps drop the ward, and the country at\nhome). For the point in step 1 (bearing 116°, so south-east):"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#put-the-label-together","content":"The units are the same for every point in them, so an app tracking a moving user can keep the\nlast answer until the point leaves the lowest unit."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#everything-within-a-radius","content":"For \"what is around me\" lists, `within_radius` gives every place within a distance, nearest\nfirst, with the same columns:"},"1952":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-15","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#everything-within-a-radius","content":"<TourCode region=\"radius\" />"},"1953":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-16","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#next","content":"Reverse geocoding and admin units in full."},"1954":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place-17","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place#next","content":"Outlines on a map: draw the unit the user is in."},"1955":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","type":"page","content":"In the browser","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser"},"1956":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","content":"Run the WebAssembly engine in a page or a Web Worker, cache the pack for offline visits, and answer every query locally."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#install","content":"Install"},"1958":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#load-the-module-and-a-pack","content":"Load the module and a pack"},"1959":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#keep-the-pack-for-offline-visits","content":"Keep the pack for offline visits"},"1960":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#query-off-the-main-thread-for-large-packs","content":"Query, off the main thread for large packs"},"1961":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#next","content":"Next"},"1962":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","content":"`teigeodata-wasm` is the engine compiled to WebAssembly: the same calls as the\nNode.js package, at most 256 KiB gzipped, or 150 KiB without name search. A page has no file\nsystem, so it opens a pack from its bytes; after that one download, every query runs locally.\nThe playground is this package in a page."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#install","content":"Install the package as the install page shows (the\n**Browser** tab). Bundlers (Vite, webpack, esbuild) treat the `.wasm` file as an asset, and the\npackage's `exports` pick the right entry for browsers, Node.js, Deno and Cloudflare Workers."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#load-the-module-and-a-pack","content":"`init()` loads and compiles the module once; then open the pack's bytes:"},"1965":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#keep-the-pack-for-offline-visits","content":"Serve the pack with a long `Cache-Control` and a content hash in its name, so a later visit\nopens it with no network. To install packs as the user needs them, verified and resumable, use\nthe browser store (`teigeodata-wasm/store`): see the store."},"1966":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#query-off-the-main-thread-for-large-packs","content":"Every query of the other languages works here:"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#query-off-the-main-thread-for-large-packs","content":"<TourCode region=\"reverse\" />"},"1968":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#query-off-the-main-thread-for-large-packs","content":"Opening copies the bytes into the module's memory once; queries then take microseconds. For a\npack of tens of megabytes, open it in a Web Worker so the page stays responsive while it loads."},"1969":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#next","content":"Install, the **Browser** tab: `npm install teigeodata-wasm`,\nor a build from a checkout."},"1970":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser#next","content":"Measured: the module sizes and their budgets."},"1971":{"id":"/docs/tutorials","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials"},"1972":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"Step-by-step walkthroughs of what apps build with teigeodata, each in every language, with code that ran."},"1973":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"Each walkthrough takes one thing an app needs and builds it step by step, with every snippet\nin every language. The code is cut from programs that ran on the released pack, so what you\ncopy works."},"1974":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"<Card title=\"A place search box\" href=\"/docs/tutorials/search-box\" description=\"Autocomplete that finds काठमाडौं, Kathmandu and Kathamandu, near the user first.\" />"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"<Card title=\"A GPS fix to a place\" href=\"/docs/tutorials/gps-to-place\" description=\"“331 m SE of Narayanhiti Royal Palace · Kathmandu, Bagmati” from a latitude and longitude.\" />"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"<Card title=\"Local time anywhere\" href=\"/docs/tutorials/local-time\" description=\"The zone, the offset and daylight saving at any point, without the device's clock rules.\" />"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"<Card title=\"Outlines on a map\" href=\"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines\" description=\"The district a user is in, drawn on MapLibre or Leaflet.\" />"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"<Card title=\"In the browser\" href=\"/docs/tutorials/in-the-browser\" description=\"The WebAssembly engine in a page or a worker, offline after one download.\" />"},"1979":{"id":"/docs/tutorials-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials","content":"<Card title=\"Replace a Nominatim server\" href=\"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim\" description=\"Nominatim-compatible search and reverse from one pack and a few megabytes of memory.\" />"},"1980":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","type":"page","content":"Local time anywhere","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time"},"1981":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","content":"The IANA timezone at any point, its UTC offset at any instant, and whether a local time is skipped or repeated, from the tzdb rules in the pack rather than the device's."},"1982":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#the-zone-at-a-point","content":"The zone at a point"},"1983":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#the-offset-at-an-instant","content":"The offset at an instant"},"1984":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#check-that-a-local-time-exists","content":"Check that a local time exists"},"1985":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#know-how-fresh-the-rules-are","content":"Know how fresh the rules are"},"1986":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#next","content":"Next"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","content":"A calendar, a prayer-time or sunrise app, a booking form: each needs the local time *at a\nplace*, not on the device. teigeodata answers it from the pack alone: the zone boundaries say\nwhich zone a point is in, and the tzdb rules carried in the pack say its offset at any instant,\nso an old phone with stale rules still gets it right."},"1988":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#the-zone-at-a-point","content":"`tz_at` gives the IANA zone the point falls in, as the zone boundaries draw it. Every place\nalso carries its zone in its `tz` column, so a place picked from search needs no second query."},"1989":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#the-offset-at-an-instant","content":"With the zone, `tz_offset_at` gives the UTC offset at a Unix time: seconds east of UTC, the\nabbreviation, and whether daylight saving is in force. Nepal is `+0545` all year; New York\nchanges twice a year."},"1990":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#check-that-a-local-time-exists","content":"When a user picks a wall-clock time, `tz_local` says whether it happens once (`unique`), never\n(`gap`: the clocks skipped it) or twice (`overlap`: the clocks went back), with the offsets\nbefore and after. A booking form should refuse the gap and ask which of the two in an overlap."},"1991":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#check-that-a-local-time-exists","content":"The three steps, in each language:"},"1992":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#check-that-a-local-time-exists","content":"<TourCode region=\"timezone\" />"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#know-how-fresh-the-rules-are","content":"`tz_version` names the tzdb release the rules come from (`2026f` above). Governments change\ntheir rules with short notice; a newer data release carries the new rules, and the\nstore updates installed packs."},"1994":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#next","content":"Timezones in full."},"1995":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/local-time","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/local-time#next","content":"Elevation: with the height of the point, sunrise and sunset times\ncan account for the horizon dip."},"1996":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","type":"page","content":"Outlines on a map","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines"},"1997":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","content":"Find the admin unit a user is in and draw its outline on MapLibre, Leaflet or OpenLayers, at a level of detail that suits the zoom."},"1998":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#find-the-unit","content":"Find the unit"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#get-its-outline","content":"Get its outline"},"2000":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#draw-it","content":"Draw it"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#next","content":"Next"},"2002":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","content":"Show the user the district or municipality they are in, outlined on a map. The outline comes\nfrom the pack as a GeoJSON Feature, the format every web map library reads."},"2003":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#find-the-unit","content":"`admin_at` gives every unit that holds the point, country first. Pick the level you want to\ndraw: a district for an overview, the lowest level for a neighbourhood."},"2004":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#find-the-unit","content":"<TourCode region=\"admin\" />"},"2005":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#get-its-outline","content":"`boundary` answers the unit's outline as one row: `geojson` (an RFC 7946 Feature), the `lod`\nread, its `tolerance_m` and the number of `vertices`. Level 0 is full resolution; 1 to 6 are\ncoarser, and neighbouring units still share their borders at every level."},"2006":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#get-its-outline","content":"<TourCode region=\"boundary\" />"},"2007":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#get-its-outline","content":"A country at level 0 can be megabytes: draw a coarser level, and keep 0 for measuring."},"2008":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#draw-it","content":"The Feature goes to the map as it is. In MapLibre GL JS:"},"2009":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#draw-it","content":"Leaflet's `L.geoJSON(feature)`, OpenLayers' `GeoJSON` format, Shapely's `shape()`, Turf and\nJTS read it too. The playground draws outlines this way."},"2010":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#next","content":"Outlines in full, with every level of detail."},"2011":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/map-outlines#next","content":"Accuracy: where the boundaries come\nfrom, and the perspective on disputed ones."},"2012":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","type":"page","content":"Replace a Nominatim server","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim"},"2013":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","content":"Serve Nominatim-compatible search, reverse and lookup from one pack with tgd serve, so existing clients keep working, using a few megabytes of memory."},"2014":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#install-the-command-line","content":"Install the command line"},"2015":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#get-a-pack-and-serve-it","content":"Get a pack and serve it"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#point-the-client-at-it","content":"Point the client at it"},"2017":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#check-what-it-holds","content":"Check what it holds"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","content":"Clients written for Nominatim (geocoder plugins, mobile SDKs, scripts) call `/search`,\n`/reverse` and `/lookup`. `tgd serve` answers those routes from a pack, alongside its own JSON\nand GeoJSON API, with no database to import and no rate limit but your own."},"2019":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#get-a-pack-and-serve-it","content":"The server binds to loopback by default; put it behind your reverse proxy to expose it."},"2020":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#point-the-client-at-it","content":"Clients set Nominatim's base URL to the server's. A search, as a client sends it:"},"2021":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#check-what-it-holds","content":"`/search` takes `q` or the structured fields, `limit` (at most 40, as Nominatim),\n`countrycodes`, `viewbox` and `bounded`; `/reverse` takes `lat`, `lon` and `zoom`. The\nHTTP server page lists every parameter and\nwhat differs from Nominatim."},"2022":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/replace-nominatim#check-what-it-holds","content":"Measured on loopback, a pack answers tens of thousands of Nominatim searches a second with a\npeak footprint of about 16 MB (server load)."},"2023":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"page","content":"A place search box","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Use cases"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box"},"2024":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","content":"Build an autocomplete for places that understands every script and spelling, ranks places near the user first, and answers each keystroke in about a millisecond on a phone."},"2025":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#open-the-pack-once","content":"Open the pack once"},"2026":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#search-on-each-keystroke","content":"Search on each keystroke"},"2027":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#rank-the-places-near-the-user-first","content":"Rank the places near the user first"},"2028":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#let-users-type-the-parent","content":"Let users type the parent"},"2029":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#label-each-row","content":"Label each row"},"2030":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#next","content":"Next"},"2031":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","content":"A user types into a box; under it, the right places appear as they type. This walkthrough\nbuilds that box's engine side: one engine for the app, a query per keystroke, near places\nfirst, and each row labelled with the units that hold it."},"2032":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#open-the-pack-once","content":"Open the engine when the app starts and keep it: opening maps the file and checks its head,\nand each section is verified the first time a query reads it. One engine answers every\nthread, so the search box and the rest of the app share it."},"2033":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#open-the-pack-once","content":"<TourCode region=\"open\" />"},"2034":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#search-on-each-keystroke","content":"Pass what the user has typed so far. Search matches every name of every place, in every\nscript, and forgives typos, missing accents and abbreviations, so the box needs no\nnormalisation of its own. Ask for the few rows the box shows."},"2035":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#search-on-each-keystroke","content":"<TourCode region=\"search\" />"},"2036":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#search-on-each-keystroke","content":"On a low-end phone a keystroke is estimated at 1.3 ms at the 99th percentile\n(measured): fast enough to run on every keystroke\nwithout a debounce, though off the UI thread is still the right place for it."},"2037":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#rank-the-places-near-the-user-first","content":"Many places share a name. Pass the user's location (or the middle of the map they are looking\nat) as the **focus**: places near it rank first, the gain halving with every `focus_scale_m`\nof distance."},"2038":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#rank-the-places-near-the-user-first","content":"<TourCode region=\"search-focus\" />"},"2039":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-15","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#rank-the-places-near-the-user-first","content":"For an autocomplete where an exact name must always be on top, set `strict`: rows rank by how\nwell the name matched (exact, loose, prefix, one edit, substring, two edits, phonetic), then\nby importance and focus."},"2040":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-16","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#let-users-type-the-parent","content":"People write \"Kathmandu, Bagmati\" to mean the one in Bagmati. Search reads the part after the\ncomma as an ancestor of any level and ranks places inside it first; the box passes the text as\nit is."},"2041":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-17","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#let-users-type-the-parent","content":"<TourCode region=\"search-parent\" />"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-18","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#label-each-row","content":"Two rows called \"Pokhara\" need their district and province to tell them apart. The ancestors\nof a place give the label, parent first:"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-19","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#label-each-row","content":"<TourCode region=\"hierarchy\" />"},"2044":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-20","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#label-each-row","content":"Show the name and the first one or two ancestors (\"Pokhara · Kaski, Gandaki\"). When the user\npicks a row, store its `id`: IDs survive data updates, and a merged place's old ID answers\nwith the survivor."},"2045":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-21","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#next","content":"Search in full: every option and column."},"2046":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box-22","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/search-box","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/search-box#next","content":"A GPS fix to a place: the reverse of this box."}},"count":2046},"sorting":{"enabled":false},"pinning":{"rules":[]},"language":"english","store":{"exclude":[],"vectors":false}}